  "image_name": "2b43747f67a1bfe515c9a83af39073f8.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure presents the immune system as a central hub that directs different kinds of cells to specific tissues and biological settings through chemokine receptor–ligand pairs. From the center, arrows radiate to major contexts such as development or tissue repair, acute inflammation, infections by intracellular or extracellular pathogens, responses to macroparasites, residence in non-lymphoid tissues, formation of lymphoid tissues, and cancer.\n\nEach surrounding box links a tissue or disease state with the immune cell types involved and the chemokine signals that appear to guide their movement. For example, acute inflammation highlights monocytes and macrophages, while intracellular pathogens are associated with T helper 17 cells, cytotoxic lymphocytes, NK cells, and related subsets. Extracellular bacteria and fungi emphasize neutrophils and other innate or helper populations, and macroparasites are tied to type 2 immunity, including eosinophils and mast cells. Other boxes show chemokine programs for stem or repair-associated cells, memory lymphocytes in peripheral tissues, and organized lymphoid structures.\n\nA cancer panel sits above the center with a question mark, suggesting a less clearly defined or more complex relationship between immune trafficking cues and malignancy compared with the other, better characterized settings.",
